     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "```json\n{\"unit_id\": \"Nubemhat\", \"choices\": [], \"reasoning\": \"Record 1, Nubemhat, is the conventional modern spelling of Nbw-m-ḥꜣ.t, 'gold is in front'. 'Nub' is the standard rendering of nbw 'gold' (as in Nubkheperre or Nubnefer), and it is distinct from 'Neb' for nb 'lord'. The rest of the name is the preposition m ('em') and ḥꜣ.t 'front' ('hat', with a for ꜣ). C1, Nb-m-ht ('Neb-em-het'): its words are nb 'lord' + m + ht. The bare consonants n-b-m-h-t fit, but its translation gives Neb, not Nub, so its first word is nb, not nbw 'gold'. Its final word is ht with plain h, not ḥꜣ.t. Record 1 renders different words, so this is not the same name. C2, Nb-mḥy.t ('Neb-mehyt'): its words are nb + the goddess name Mḥy.t, with no separate preposition. Record 1 renders nbw 'gold' + m + ḥꜣ.t, so the words differ and this is not the same name. C3, Nb=j-m-jꜥḥ ('Nebi-em-jah'): its words are nb=j 'my lord' + m + jꜥḥ 'moon'. The candidate has no t, so the final t of Nubemhat would be an added consonant, and the words also differ. Not the same name. Review X's acceptance of C1 rests on consonants alone and ignores that Nub and Neb are different words. Review Y is correct.\"}\n```"
     }
